WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:18.133
Antibiotics are substances made by microorganisms, mainly fungi such as Penicillium, and some bacteria. An antibiotic kills bacteria, or stops them growing. So antibiotics treat bacterial diseases, such as pneumonia, cholera, gonorrhoea and TB.

00:00:18.700 --> 00:00:34.807
Antibiotics work on bacteria only. Viruses live inside our own cells, and they do not have the parts that antibiotics attack. So antibiotics do not treat Ebola or flu. They do not treat fungal infections, like athlete's foot, either.

00:00:37.700 --> 00:00:54.681
Now resistance. Resistance means the bacteria are not killed by the antibiotic. It starts by chance. A random mutation, a change in the DNA of one bacterium, makes it resistant. The mutation happens before the antibiotic is used.

00:00:55.250 --> 00:01:02.567
Then the antibiotic is used. It kills the bacteria that are not resistant. The resistant bacterium survives.

00:01:03.150 --> 00:01:11.705
The survivor reproduces, and passes on the resistance gene. Over many generations, most of the population becomes resistant.

00:01:12.300 --> 00:01:30.263
Overuse of antibiotics, and patients who do not finish the course, help resistant bacteria to be selected. Resistant infections are hard to treat. MRSA is a strain of Staphylococcus aureus that resists many antibiotics, and it spreads in hospitals.

00:01:31.400 --> 00:01:39.464
Here is a real exam question. Explain why it is not possible to treat athlete's foot using antibiotics. Two marks.

00:01:40.050 --> 00:01:48.562
First, the cause. Athlete's foot is caused by a fungus, not a bacterium. Second, antibiotics only act on bacteria.

00:01:50.850 --> 00:02:00.215
Link them. Antibiotics have no effect on fungi, so they cannot cure athlete's foot. Answer by olevels.com, not an official mark scheme.

00:02:00.800 --> 00:02:02.933
So the answer has two linked ideas.

00:02:04.150 --> 00:02:14.070
A common mistake is to say bacteria become immune to an antibiotic. Immunity is about antibodies in your body. Bacteria become resistant.

00:02:14.650 --> 00:02:24.719
Another mistake is to say the antibiotic causes the mutation. The mutation is random, and it happens first. The antibiotic only kills the others.

00:02:25.850 --> 00:02:44.410
Your turn. Sara has a throat infection caused by bacteria. She stops taking her antibiotics after two days, because she feels better. Explain how this could lead to resistant bacteria. Then explain why a doctor will not give her antibiotics for flu. Pause here and work it out.

00:02:45.550 --> 00:02:57.838
After two days, the antibiotic has killed the bacteria that are easy to kill. The most resistant ones are still alive. If she stops, they survive, reproduce and pass on their resistance.

00:02:58.550 --> 00:03:07.062
Flu is caused by a virus. Antibiotics only work on bacteria, so they would have no effect, and using them adds to overuse.

00:03:07.650 --> 00:03:20.493
So, every time: check the pathogen type. Antibiotics act on bacteria only. For resistance: mutation, the others die, survivors reproduce, the population becomes resistant.
